// Martial Arts: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== martial-arts.f01 | feature | three-act
title: Roda
genre: martial-arts + historical-drama
tone: Percussive, sly and defiant
era: 1890 | period
setting: The docks and hill quarters of Rio de Janeiro | real | city
logline: The year the new republic makes capoeira a crime punishable by exile, a dockworker who is the best fighter in his gang must decide whether the art survives by going underground or by learning to look like a dance.
premise: Slavery was abolished two years ago. The republic's first penal code names one practice of the freed by name: to be seen doing "agility exercises known as capoeiragem" is six months on a prison island. The police chief means to clear the streets. {canario}, a stevedore, has never lost in the circle. His gang wants him to fight the police. An old woman wants him to teach her grandchildren.
synopsis: The first act is the art in the open: a ring of men on a quay at dusk, one stringed bow, two players moving as if joking, until one is on his back. {canario} is quick, vain and loyal to the gang that fed him. The decree is read. That week the chief, {vidal}, raids the docks with cavalry; men are shipped to the island without trial. The second act is two futures. The gang's captain, {quincas}, hires out to politicians as election muscle and wants razors in the game; that way lies money, and the island. {tia_bibiana}, who runs a house on the hill where there is always drumming on a saint's day, has another idea: play slower, play to music, call it a dance, do it in her yard with the gate open and the police welcome to watch. {canario} thinks this is surrender. He fights {vidal}'s best man in an alley and wins, and three of his friends are deported for it. In the yard, a girl of ten, {dora}, is better than any of them at the one thing he never learned: getting out of the way. He begins to see that the game is the opposite of a brawl; it is how not to be where the blow lands, for three hundred years. In the third act {vidal} comes to the yard on a feast day to arrest him. He is invited in. A circle forms, the bow starts, and {canario} plays the chief's sergeant with the whole street watching: never strikes, is never touched, and ends with the man on the ground laughing in spite of himself. There is no law against a dance. {vidal} knows exactly what he has seen and goes away. {quincas} is on the island by Christmas. The last shot is {dora}, grown, in a ring of her own.
question: Is it still resistance if it survives by smiling?
themes: disguise as strategy; who inherits an art; the fight you win by not being hit
audience: Adults and teenagers; martial-arts fans who want history with it
viewpoint: With {canario}; fights are filmed whole, in the circle, without cuts to hide them
stakes: Exile for anyone caught, the soul of an art, and the children in a yard
conflict: A fighter's pride against a state that has outlawed his body, and against the two ways his own people offer to survive it
ending: He beats the law by dancing in front of it. The street fighters are deported; the yard goes on.
experience: The joy of bodies doing the impossible, anger with somewhere to go, and a final bout that is all wit
look: Dusk on wet quays, white clothes kept clean in a fight, a hill yard strung with paper flags
sound: One berimbau, hand claps, call and answer. Hooves on cobbles when the cavalry comes.
m: keep | system | order-vs-change | triumph
@ canario | Canario | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 24 | To stay unbeaten and unowned | The circle is the only place he is somebody | What he is good at is now a crime | Vain, funny, fearless, slow to think ahead | From brawler to the art's best disguise | Dock slang, sung boasts | White suit, bare feet, a razor he stops carrying
@ tia_bibiana | Tia Bibiana | co-lead | mentor | human | she/her | 46 | The children safe and the old ways alive | She has kept a house open through worse | The young men think patience is cowardice | Regal, shrewd, hospitable as a tactic | From offering him a way to watching him take it | Bahian warmth, gives orders as invitations | White lace, beads, a turban
@ vidal | Chief Honorio Vidal | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 43 | Clean streets for the republic | He believes order is progress | The law names a thing he cannot define | Efficient, vain, not stupid | From raids to a silent withdrawal | Clipped, official | Uniform, kid gloves, a riding crop
@ quincas | Quincas | supporting | rival | human | he/him | 35 | The gang paid and feared | He remembers being property | His road ends on an island | Hard, generous to his own, done with patience | From captain to deportee | Low, threatening, loyal | Hat tilted, a razor in the hatband
@ dora | Dora | featured | foil | human | she/her | 10 | To play | Nobody has told her she cannot | She is small | Fearless, quick, amused by adults | From pupil to inheritor | Giggles; sings the responses | Cotton dress, cartwheels everywhere
~ canario - tia_bibiana | fighter and matriarch | he calls it surrender | he plays in her yard
~ canario - quincas | best man and captain | loyalty | a razor handed back
~ canario - vidal | outlaw and police chief | prey | a performance the chief cannot arrest
~ canario - dora | champion and child | she is underfoot | she teaches him to not be there
